{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfdDnFkRf5Qh7jw5pCLwC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- **Logic in the right unit.** The structured event is modelled as its own immutable `record OwnerCreatedEvent` with a `from(seq, owner)` factory that owns field mapping; `AuditOwnerCreated` keeps the cross-cutting concerns that genuinely belong to the step (the shared `SEQUENCE`, the `JsonMapper`, and emission on the `AUDIT` logger). That's a natural split — the record stays a pure value, the function stays the side-effecting audit step.\n\n- **No catch-all.** `AuditOwnerCreated` already exists to audit owner creation; emitting both the human line and the structured event is the same single responsibility (audit of a created owner, same logger), so folding the event in rather than inventing a second step is the right call and doesn't bloat the class into something unfocused.\n\n- **No meaningful duplication.** The overlap between the audit line's field list and the record's components is incidental — the line carries extra fields (registrationDate, membershipNumber) and serves a different (human) purpose; abstracting a shared field source here would couple two formats that legitimately differ.\n\n- **Forward-compat handled in the right place.** Sourcing the primary identifier through `Owner.getCustomerCode()` in a single factory means the memberId-unification future works itself out with no change here, and the Javadoc documents that single source of truth clearly — exactly the kind of note a maintainer of this repo would want."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10423,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10423},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standa